"""The welcome film every account sees once, at its first login.

Full screen, about thirty seconds, skippable with any key or click. The
portlin mark draws itself, then measures itself against the drive it is
running from: its four bars take this stick's real partition sizes and each
one is labelled the way lsblk would. Its root bar pulls away into a field of
streaks, "welcome" flies past in the languages the installed fonts can draw,
and the last one to arrive is in the language the account was set up with. It
settles over the root bar again -- the wallpaper's own picture of root running
off the edge of the drive -- while the machine the stick is plugged into
introduces itself underneath, and the window fades away onto the desktop.

Root is crimson only when it is encrypted, as it is everywhere else portlin
draws it. Everything about the drive and the machine is read at start, from
the running system, because both change every time the stick moves.

Started by /etc/xdg/autostart with --once, which plays it only if this account
has never seen it, and holds it back while the open nouveau driver runs the
screen, which cannot keep up with it. Run by hand, with no arguments, it plays
again, whatever the driver.

The whole picture is a pure function of the clock: Film.draw(cr, w, h, t)
paints frame t onto any cairo context. The window is only a clock and a
surface, which is what lets the frames be rendered and looked at without an X
server, and what lets a slow machine drop frames without falling behind.
"""

